{
  "abstract": "Can components that improve rule-support retrieval also choose useful label corrections? We evaluate hierarchical query-reference calibration and answer-vocabulary restriction from two CATT attribution methods on a pretrained sentiment classifier. Each policy audits 128 of 1,280 sentences with 20% synthetic label errors, then retrains Qwen3.5-4B rank-eight LoRA for 240 matched updates. Two fixed corruption/training pairs and 1,024 reserved sentence groups support an independent finite-repair evaluation. Pairing hypothetical binary labels changes centered Z calibration to a zero-mean RMS reference while preserving the gradient factors and later pooling. It finds 103/121 corrupted labels versus 32/40 for ordinary margin Z, yet its repair accuracy is lower in both pairs. The mean difference, −0.879 percentage points with simultaneous 95% interval [−2.518, 0.761], is unresolved. Against stronger alternatives, RMS trails base conditional source loss by 1.953 points [0.394, 3.512], projected empirical Fisher by 2.148 points [0.480, 3.817], and random auditing by 1.904 points [0.180, 3.629]. No tested component adaptation passes the declared superiority rule against all four baselines. Clean parents establish test headroom. A separate 46.07-second base-loss scan reproduces both selections exactly; joint gradient collection costs are reported without inventing isolated method timings. Public evidence reconstructs all outcomes and the 38 simultaneous contrasts. The contribution is a measured limit on this component transfer: improved corruption detection does not establish a useful repair policy. Synthetic noise, perfect audits, one model family and two fixed pairs limit generality; intervals do not cover a population of training seeds.",
